# keur quality control — a Java repo whose sonar-project.properties configures
# no JaCoCo coverage report path (SonarQube/SonarCloud then shows 0% coverage).
#
# Why this exists: coverage only appears in Sonar when a LANGUAGE-SPECIFIC report
# path points at a report your test run produced. For Java/Kotlin that key is
# `sonar.coverage.jacoco.xmlReportPaths`. Miss it and the dashboard reads 0%, and
# any coverage-based quality gate fails silently — nothing else warns you.
#
# HOW (requires + absence): `requires: lang: java` gates the rule to repos that
# actually contain Java (facts from .java files / pom.xml, ADR-009) — so a JS or
# Go repo never pays for it. Within those, the absence engine fires when the
# JaCoCo key appears NOWHERE in the file. `content_contains: sonar.sources`
# skips a properties file that configures no analysis at all.
#
# This is the per-language refinement of the generic "no coverage path" idea:
# because keur knows the repo is Java (facts), it can demand the RIGHT key.
#
# WARN, low confidence: a repo may pass the path via CI (-Dsonar.coverage...) or
# deliberately skip coverage; the fix shows the exact key. @see docs/rules-authoring.md
id: SONAR-005
ilities: [testability]
maturity: 3
title: Java repo — Sonar config has no JaCoCo coverage report path
category: quality
severity: warning
engine: absence
action: warn
confidence: 0.5
requires:
  lang: java
target:
  filenames: sonar-project.properties
  content_contains: sonar.sources
patterns:
  - regex: (?mi)^\s*sonar\.coverage\.jacoco\.xmlReportPaths\s*=\s*\S
    message: "Java repo: sonar-project.properties sets sonar.sources but no sonar.coverage.jacoco.xmlReportPaths — Sonar will show 0% coverage. Point it at your JaCoCo XML report."
fix: "Add `sonar.coverage.jacoco.xmlReportPaths=target/site/jacoco/jacoco.xml` (Maven) or `build/reports/jacoco/test/jacocoTestReport.xml` (Gradle), and ensure the JaCoCo report is generated before the scan."
